<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikolai Yezhov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Nikolai Ivanovich Yezhov, Ezhov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikolai Yezhov was a Soviet secret police official who served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> as head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> from 1936 to 1938, at the height of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a>. A former tailor's assistant and factory worker who joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> in 1917, he rose through the Party's personnel apparatus to the Central Committee. As head of the NKVD he organised mass arrests, torture and executions: local troikas condemned ordinary citizens to fill arbitrary quotas, and he purged the security organs themselves. Lavrenty Beria, named his deputy in August 1938, took control of the NKVD and succeeded him that November. Yezhov was arrested in April 1939 and executed in 1940.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov.jpg" alt="Nikolai Yezhov" width="330" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ezhov_NI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Women were among the prisoners: Yevgenia Ginzburg recalled that the inmates could tell when <b>Nikolai Yezhov</b> no longer headed the NKVD, because one day conditions eased.</blockquote><a href="/societies/soviet-union">Social Hierarchy of Stalin's Soviet Union</a></li></ul></section>
